00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.790
MANU BATRA: Thanks, Apparao,
for the introduction.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:10.080
Now, let's look at the evolution
of storage in Kubernetes.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.950
We have seen terrific
customer adoption of GKE

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.290
for all types of workloads.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.360
This includes
stateful workloads,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.840
like databases, that were
uncommon in the earlier

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.120
waves of Kubernetes adoption.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.850
With enhancements in
Kubernetes storage support,

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.760
customers are more
comfortable deploying

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.140
data-intensive workloads, like
databases inside containers.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.750
The net result is
that GKE customers

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.260
are managing far more critical
data inside containers

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.650
than ever before.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.690
But many Cloud
customers are still

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.850
held back from
greater adoption by

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.040
their service-level objectives
and backup requirements.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.220
Additionally, we have
heard from customers

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.210
in more regulated verticals,
like finance, payments,

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.310
manufacturing, and
aerospace, that

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.800
have strict data backup
and lifecycle management

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.150
requirements.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.040
These customers
are often blocked

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.800
from expanding their
use of GKE to include

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.970
production-critical
straightforward workflows.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.640
Our goal is to unblock
traditional enterprise

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.810
customers from running more
stateful workloads with more

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.210
regulated data
inside GKE customers

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.090
and meet their backup
and BR service levels.

00:02:27.342 --> 00:02:29.760
With that objective
in mind, we're

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.260
introducing a new Google
service, called Backup for GKE.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.260
Backup for GKE is an
easy Cloud-native way

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.190
for customers running workloads
on GKE to protect, manage,

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.510
and restore their containerized
applications and data.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.680
Now, let's get
into more details,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.710
into what really
is Backup for GKE

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.600
and a bit more of
the architecture.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.330
The service operates at
three different layers.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:02.090
The bottom-most layer
is the data layer where

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.830
we take a copy of the data.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.080
Today, we do support
Persistent Disk,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.290
which is Google
Cloud's block storage.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.330
In future, we will
also be supporting

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.880
additional types of
storage, including

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.510
file and other elements.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.440
So the first part is we
take a copy of the data

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.170
and store it as a
part of the backup.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.790
Second is, as a
part of the backup,

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.350
we also take Kubernetes objects.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:32.030
And this is where a user can
define the scope of a backup.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.360
A user can define a backup
to be an entire cluster,

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.850
a specific namespace
within a cluster,

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.530
or a set of namespaces.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:43.550
Or a user can define a lot
more granular control and scope

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.410
of backup call applications.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.300
And I'll talk more
about applications

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.780
in the following slides.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.680
But once the user defines
the scope of backup,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.610
we pick up all the Kubernetes
configurations and objects,

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.370
depending on the
scope of the backup.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.120
We also orchestrate backups
within an application.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.510
And this is to support
app-consistent backups.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.670
A lot of applications
are crash-consistent,

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.690
but some applications like
to flush their memory,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:15.320
or do QS-ing and un-QS-ing
pre- and post-backup.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.399
To be able to accommodate
such applications,

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:23.360
there is a flexibility to create
application profiles, which

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.440
enables users to inject
scripts at par level for QS-ing

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.440
or other application-level
orchestration.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.270
So as I've touched,
backup essentially

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.080
includes two different elements.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.150
One is Kubernetes objects, and
second is the volume backup,

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.270
which is the Persistent
Disk data that we backup

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.740
as a part of the backup itself.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:52.970
Now, as a part of backup,
there are multiple options.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.190
As a user, you can choose
where the backup is stored.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:59.240
So if there are data
residency requirements,

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.180
you can specify which region you
want the backups to be stored.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.830
You also have ability to select
and skip certain resources.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.230
If you're managing
secrets outside,

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.950
you can tell the backup service
to skip and not backup secrets

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.200
as a part of the backup package.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.230
Same thing, you can also
decide not to backup volumes,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.810
if there are other ways
you are backing up data,

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.430
or it's more for transient data
that you don't want to backup.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.440
Another feature,
which is very critical

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.230
to protect against ransomware
and malicious deletions,

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.120
is time lock.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.070
The service allows you
to time lock backups,

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:38.900
which will disable any
automated or manual deletion

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.910
of the backup, unless
the time lock expires.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.610
This is a really
critical feature,

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.110
in case a ransomware
attack, or somebody

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:51.440
tries to delete those backups
with a malicious intent.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:57.910
And of course, all data
is encrypted, by default.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.850
Getting more into details
about protected applications.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.020
So I touched upon a user can
define the scope of backup.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.510
A user can define a
cluster as a scope

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.790
of backup, a set of namespaces.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:13.240
Or, depending on
the scope of backup,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.760
a user can define a
specific application.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.530
And an application
can correspond

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:21.790
to something like a
MySQL, or a Redis Data,

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:23.800
or a Postgres database itself.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.030
And this allows users to execute
QS-ing and un-QS-ing commands

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.610
pre- and post-backup.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:34.160
We also support more complex
definitions of applications,

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.680
like an HR data
processing application,

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:40.240
which could be a combination
of a messaging bus, a database,

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.310
and a web services agent.

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:45.310
The system allows you to
group all those applications

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:47.140
into protected
application groups.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.050
And the backup
orchestrates, knowing

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.590
that a particular application
is part of a larger component,

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:57.310
and orchestrates the backup
with that knowledge in mind.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.500
So essentially, as a
part of the backup,

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:03.310
you can define a cluster, a
namespace, or an application

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.950
or application group as
a scope of the backup.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.630
Backups are fine,
recoveries are better.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.960
This is where the tool
provides multiple use

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.350
cases to restore the data.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:21.920
You can backup a cluster,
restore the cluster

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.290
into a new cluster,
or into a new region.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.710
The system also allows
you to backup a cluster

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:30.920
and restore a specific
namespace or an application.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.920
This can be done in case there
was an accidental deletion

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.010
of a specific data set,
or an upgrade failed

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:41.160
and you want to restore the data
in its previous healthy state.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.240
The system also allows
you to clone and scale

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.870
workloads in different regions.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.140
So all these are
multiple use cases

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:53.070
that can be supported via
the backup and the recovery

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.730
workflows.

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:59.750
Getting into a bit more
details of restore options.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:02.060
As a part of the
restore, the system

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:05.540
also allows users to
change certain parameters

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:07.470
before the backup is restored.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.610
This may include changing
storage classes or replica

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:11.240
accounts.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.720
An example could be, you backup
production environment, which

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.370
was running PDS SDs.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:19.700
But for test and dev, you
are using PD Standard.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.920
So this is where,
before you restore,

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.730
you can change storage classes
to map to the new environment

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:29.310
that you are restoring.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.690
Another use case is sub-scope
restores where you can always

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:35.250
backup an entire cluster.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:37.559
But at the time of
restore, you can decide

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:39.179
what is the scope of restore.

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:41.370
You may restore
only one namespace,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.650
or a set of namespaces,
or one application out

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.020
of the entire backup.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.540
Another feature that
was added to the service

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.160
is for delegated admin.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.160
And this use case is
more around when--

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.010
let's say a cluster backup
executes at midnight.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.650
But as an app
admin, you may want

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.680
to take an ad hoc
backup at 4:00 PM,

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.720
just before a critical
upgrade is kicking in.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.670
So this allows cluster admins
to delegate certain authority

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:13.200
to application admins to
execute an ad hoc backup just

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.660
before critical upgrades
or critical changes that

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.510
are about to make an
application, namespace,

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:19.140
or a cluster.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.620
Backup for GKE experience is
completely integrated into GKE.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:30.350
So as you log into
your GKE framework,

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:34.830
you'll be able to see Backup and
Restore in the left navigation.

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.980
Once you get in there,
you'll be able to see

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.430
all the backup policies, all
the clusters that are protected

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:44.540
with the backup policies, when
was the last backup executed,

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.820
and what was the
duration of the backup.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:50.240
The whole interface also allows
you to create new backup plans,

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.820
restore plans, the
intent being it's

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.490
a completely seamless
workflow as you

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:59.280
operate your workloads in GKE.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.070
Now, I'll pass to
Apparao to talk more

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:07.480
about Broadcom's experience
using Backup for GKE service.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:08.530
Apparao?

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.800
APPARAO CHITIKELA: Thanks, Manu.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.690
So I'm part of Broadcom team.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.940
So my team is Saas Platform.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:19.420
So this thing, this Cloud,
it shows as the platform.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:22.950
So in the platform, we
have different components.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:26.550
Those are infrastructure,
monitoring, Kubernetes,

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:30.480
networking, identity,
and access management.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.260
While using this platform,
different products teams

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.090
will deploy their applications.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:41.850
So Broadcom software uses a
foundational Saas Platform

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.560
architecture to manage multiple
elements across regions

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:49.110
and lifecycle stages.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.920
The core functional
uses Terraform modules

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.550
to describe the infrastructure,
node, networking,

00:10:56.550 --> 00:11:00.780
and Kubernetes cluster used
to support Saas products being

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.640
delivered to the customers.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.330
The environment spans
across development, verify,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:10.500
and production, and spread
across multiple regions.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.370
By using flexible modules,
we can create clusters

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:17.790
to various sizes, while
maintaining consistency

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.290
across environments.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:23.090
Yeah.

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:26.240
This is-- it will give you
more details about the host

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.250
projects and shared VPC.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:31.610
All service projects are
connected with the Shared VPC

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:36.330
project and will communicate
each of the applications.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.360
So the Saas Platform
Foundation takes

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.660
advantage of a
cross-project communication,

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:44.420
using Google's Shared VPC.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:47.450
This enables separation
between the GKE projects.

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:52.250
And product-specific projects
are projects for dB servers

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:54.795
that have not yet
been migrated to GKE.

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:04.640
Yeah, this slide talks about the
state of stateful applications.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:07.180
So as with most of
enterprise companies,

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:09.640
we started our
Kubernetes journey

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.580
with deploying
stateless applications.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:17.050
This helped us take advantage
of the GKE scale and cost

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:18.700
optimization.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:23.590
Moving to GKE improved
our development velocity,

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.590
besides helping with
optimizing cost.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.930
But the split
architecture created

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:33.280
some additional challenges,
as our application components

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:36.070
were split between
VMs and containers.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:38.680
Because they are
stateful applications,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:42.550
it takes a lot of additional
data management services

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:47.680
in the platform to trust it
with data, especially databases.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.290
Modernizing applications
during the migration

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:53.350
by moving the
operators-based versions

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:57.550
allows us to add
operational flexibility.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.430
We had stateful
applications on VMs

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:03.610
and stateless
applications on GKE,

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.330
creating application complexity.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.670
In order to simplify the
operational environment,

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:13.950
we started exploring moving
stateful applications to GKE

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:15.660
as well.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:16.490
So we'll continue.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:19.700
So these stateful
applications, today, we

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:24.080
have about 150 stateful
workloads deployed in GKE.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.780
This helps us
streamline operations

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.420
and remove a lot of
functional causes

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.760
by non-uniform involvement.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:35.210
But a few challenges
remain, likely ability

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:39.260
to issue backup and disaster
recovery service level.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:44.270
Remote some workloads to GKE,
but with very fragmented backup

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:45.590
solutions.

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:48.070
The fragment backup
solutions made

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:50.540
it difficult to
maintain consistency

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.030
and created
operational challenges.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:01.430
Key requirements for critical
stateful applications.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:04.910
Some of the use cases that will
help for key stateful workloads

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:09.450
are ability to achieve critical
backup and disaster recovery

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:13.550
service levels, ability
to recover an application

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:19.940
to non-healthy state in the
event of fault or corruption.

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:23.990
Other use cases include ability
to quickly scale and migrate

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:25.900
applications.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:29.800
Backup for GKE provides us
with an integrated backup

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:32.710
and recovery platform to
achieve these critical use

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:36.630
cases, enabling us to migrate
additional workloads to GKE.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:43.520
Key highlights from GKE backup.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.430
We were particularly
impressed with the simplicity

00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:49.790
of taking cluster backups
and ability to implement

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:54.410
standard backup policies across
our enterprise workloads.

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:58.640
Backup for GKE also supports
application-consistent backups,

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:00.920
besides crash-consistent backup.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:04.070
This enables us to break
any soils of backups

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:07.290
across our organization.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.510
Ability to take
incremental backup

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:14.835
helps us optimize cost without
compromising critical service

00:15:14.835 --> 00:15:15.335
levels.

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:23.090
So our experience on
Backup for GKE service.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:26.120
After [INAUDIBLE] introduced
us to the new offering Backup

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:30.560
for GKE, it unblocked a lot
of critical stateful workloads

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.870
to move GKE.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:37.370
Before backup, we are using
storage backup directly.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:39.410
It made it very
difficult to adhere

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:41.630
to service-level projects.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:44.300
We are using Backup
for GKE service,

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:49.190
and we'll integrate it part
of our core platform services.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:52.010
This will enable us to
standardize our backup

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:55.790
and disaster recovery policies
for the entire Kubernetes

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:58.080
application fleet.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:15:58.740
Thanks.

